Ladybird retains double-precision animation progress through value
and calculation interpolation, narrowing it only at float-based
color and SVG-path interfaces. This removes small errors in animated
angles and calculated values, and lets a typed custom property
animate with the expected value in a shadow tree.

Blink’s InterpolableValue::Interpolate accepts double progress.
WebKit’s Style::Interpolation::Context receives double progress
and passes it to BlendingContext. Gecko’s Procedure::Interpolate
stores progress as f64. Ladybird uses the same precision without
copying their interpolation code.

Chrome returns exact 36deg, 72deg, 108deg, and 144deg rotations and
calc(-20% + 12px) in the compared cases. The function-shadow-animations
WPT passes all three subtests with this precision change. Existing
easing-selection and hidden-subtree query differences remain separate;
their expectations only change in numeric precision.
